# Compose `{{ title }}` — write `src/scene.js`

The environment (`src/env.js`), zones (`src/zones/*.js`) and assets (`src/assets/*.js`,
`public/assets/*.glb`) exist. Write the entry module that wires them together.

## Contract
{{ frame_doc }}
{{ contract }}
`src/scene.js` must `export function createScene({ THREE, renderer, loaders }) → { scene, cameras, update }`:
- `scene`: a `THREE.Scene`; call `buildEnv(THREE, scene)` from `./env.js`, then add every zone group
  (`build<Zone>(ctx)` from `./zones/<zone>.js` with `ctx = { THREE, env, heightAt, MATS, rand }`),
  each zone group named after the zone.
- `cameras`: an array of `THREE.PerspectiveCamera` named after the planned cameras, positioned at the
  planned `position` and `lookAt` numbers, `fov` as planned, aspect 16:9, near 0.1, far ≥ 2 × bounds.
  `cameras[0]` is the establishing shot.
- `update(t, dt)`: calls `tickEnv(t, dt)` and every `userData.tick(t, dt)` found on zone/asset groups
  (traverse once at creation and cache the list; never traverse per frame).
- Wrap each zone build in try/catch: a failing zone logs `console.error('[zone <name>] ' + err)` and is skipped.
- Use a seeded mulberry32 `rand` (seed 1) so frames are reproducible.
- GLB assets load via `loaders.gltf.loadAsync('public/assets/<snake>.glb')` — if you load any, keep the
  scene buildable before they resolve (add to the group on resolve).

